              "end": 2,
              "start": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The plaintiffs are victims and families of victims of terrorist attacks carried out by Hamas between 2001 and 2003, and they sued BLO\u202fBank\u202fSAL under the Anti\u2011Terrorism Act for allegedly aiding and abetting the attacks by providing financial services to Hamas\u2011affiliated customers."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "29b956d5b985309b9b690ecb191c032b3d8c7afb2387c12c3a6a7acbe69652f7",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0000-0007",
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0000-0006",
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0001-0000",
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0001-0001",
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0001-0002",
              "ev-29b956d5b985-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 15,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Supreme Court reversed the Second Circuit\u2019s judgment and remanded the case, holding that relief under Rule\u202f60(b)(6) requires extraordinary circumstances and that the standard does not become less demanding when the movant also seeks to amend the complaint; the Court further held that only the Rule\u202f60(b) standard\u2014not a hybrid with Rule\u202f15(a)\u2014applies to a motion to vacate a final judgment."
      }
    ],
